---
import { renderToString } from "katex";
import EvidenceDetails from "./EvidenceDetails.astro";
import ForgetRetentionLedger from "./ForgetRetentionLedger.astro";
import JourneyMap from "./JourneyMap.astro";
import ModeSwitch from "./ModeSwitch.astro";
import { getJourneyOverview, type StageView } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { view: StageView }
const { view } = Astro.props;
const { mode } = view;
const scenario = view.forgetScenario;
if (scenario === undefined) throw new Error("Forget gates are missing their reviewed scenario.");
const expert = mode === "expert";
const journey = getJourneyOverview("recurrent-memory");
const claim = (suffix: string) => {
  const evidence = view.evidence.find((item) => item.claimId === `gers-2000-${suffix}`);
  if (evidence === undefined) throw new Error(`Missing forget-gate evidence: ${suffix}`);
  return evidence;
};
const math = (tex: string) => renderToString(tex, { displayMode: true, output: "htmlAndMathml", throwOnError: true });
const gate = math(String.raw`\mathrm{net}_{\varphi_j}(t)=\sum_m w_{\varphi_j m}y^m(t-1),\quad y^{\varphi_j}(t)=f_{\varphi_j}(\mathrm{net}_{\varphi_j}(t))`);
const state = math(String.raw`s_{c_j^v}(t)=y^{\varphi_j}(t)s_{c_j^v}(t-1)+y^{\mathrm{in}_j}(t)g(\mathrm{net}_{c_j^v}(t))`);
const eligibility = math(String.raw`\frac{\partial s_{c_j^v}(t)}{\partial w_{\varphi_j m}}=\frac{\partial s_{c_j^v}(t-1)}{\partial w_{\varphi_j m}}y^{\varphi_j}(t)+s_{c_j^v}(t-1)f'_{\varphi_j}(\mathrm{net}_{\varphi_j}(t))y^m(t-1)`);
const update = math(String.raw`\Delta w_{lm}(t)=\alpha\sum_{v=1}^{S_j}e_{s_{c_j^v}}(t)\frac{\partial s_{c_j^v}(t)}{\partial w_{lm}},\quad l\in\{\varphi_j,\mathrm{in}_j\}`);
const cerg = [
  { label: "Standard · external reset", perfect: "74%", streams: "7,441", good: "0%" },
  { label: "Standard · no reset", perfect: "0%", streams: "—", good: "1%" },
  { label: "Fixed state decay · 0.9", perfect: "0%", streams: "—", good: "0%" },
  { label: "Learned forget gate", perfect: "18%", streams: "18,889", good: "29%" },
  { label: "Forget gate + learning-rate decay", perfect: "62%", streams: "14,087", good: "6%" },
];
const cnto = [
  { label: "Standard · no reset", perfect: "0%", streams: "—", partial: "4.6" },
  { label: "Learned forget gate", perfect: "24%", streams: "18,077", partial: "12.2" },
  { label: "Forget gate + learning-rate decay", perfect: "37%", streams: "22,654", partial: "11.8" },
];
---

<article class="stage-shell forget-stage">
  <div class="stage-reading">
    <ModeSwitch activeMode={mode} alternateHref={view.alternateMode.href} />
    <header class="stage-header">
      <p class="stage-number">Stage 3 of 6 · report/proceedings 1999 · journal 2000</p>
      <h1>{expert ? "Which term does a forget gate change?" : "What survives when a cell learns to reset?"}</h1>
      <p class="stage-question">{view.contribution}</p>
      <dl class="stage-meta"><div><dt>Work</dt><dd>{view.work.title} · {view.work.authors.join(", ")}</dd></div><div><dt>Display source</dt><dd>{view.canonicalVersion.label}</dd></div><div><dt>Chronology</dt><dd>Report issue: 26 January 1999; ICANN proceedings: 1999; journal: 1 October 2000</dd></div><div><dt>Scope</dt><dd>Learned activation retention · block-shared forget gate</dd></div></dl>
    </header>
    <section class="objective-band" aria-labelledby="objective-heading"><h2 id="objective-heading">Your task</h2><p>{view.objective.objective}</p><details><summary>Success criterion</summary><p>{view.objective.successCriterion}</p></details></section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="forget-inventory">
      <p class="kicker">Mechanism inventory · 2000</p><h2 id="forget-inventory">Retain, write, expose.</h2>
      <ol class="copy-path"><li><strong>Retain</strong><span>Previous state × forget-gate activation. This replaces the original fixed multiplier of 1.0.</span></li><li><strong>Write</strong><span>Candidate × input gate. Add this after retention.</span></li><li><strong>Expose</strong><span>Transform the resulting state, then scale it by the existing output gate.</span></li></ol>
      <p class="mt-3">{expert ? "One sigmoid forget gate is shared by the cells in a memory block. The source uses scalar block/cell indices, not a modern vector equation. Its gate input is a weighted sum of source-unit outputs; it has no direct cell-state peephole." : "Keeping a running value forever is not always useful. A new topic or subsequence can make old activation irrelevant. The new gate lets the cell keep much of it, fade it, or almost remove it before accepting new input."}</p>
      <EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("forget-gate-mechanism")} />
    </section>
    {expert && <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="forget-source-equations">
      <p class="kicker">Journal equations 6–7 · internal p. 5</p><h2 id="forget-source-equations">A learned multiplier on the old state.</h2>
      <div class="equation-panel" role="region" aria-label="Journal forget-gate forward equations" tabindex="0"><div set:html={gate} /><div set:html={state} /></div>
      <p>j indexes a memory block, v a cell in that block, and m a source unit. f is logistic; g is the transformed cell input. In the ledger, “forget gate” is y φ, “previous state” is s at t−1, and “new write” is y in × g(net). The ledger supplies these activations directly and starts with populated state 0.8; the source sets s(0) = 0.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">The output equation remains y out × h(s). The comparison does not calculate an output, train a boundary detector, or show how a particular network chooses the presets.</p>
    </section>}
    {view.blocks.map((block) => <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby={block.id}><p class="kicker">{block.kind}</p><h2 id={block.id}>{block.heading}</h2><p>{block.body}</p>{block.evidence.map((evidence) => <EvidenceDetails evidence={evidence} />)}</section>)}

  </div>
  <aside class="stage-workbench stage-workbench--long" aria-label="Static fixed-write retention comparison"><ForgetRetentionLedger scenario={scenario} mode={mode} /></aside>
  <div class="stage-conclusion forget-evidence">
    {expert && <section class="reading-block forget-learning" aria-labelledby="forget-credit">
      <p class="kicker">Journal equations 17 and 20 · internal p. 8</p><h2 id="forget-credit">Carry an eligibility trace, then update shared weights.</h2>
      <p>The partial derivative below tracks a cell-state sensitivity to an incoming forget-gate weight. It is separate from the activation comparison. Under the source’s truncation, the previous sensitivity is multiplied by the current gate; the added term uses the previous cell state.</p>
      <div class="equation-panel mt-3" role="region" aria-label="Journal forget-weight eligibility recurrence" tabindex="0"><div set:html={eligibility} /></div>
      <p>For a logistic gate, f′ = y(1−y). In the idealized zero-gate limit, both the carried sensitivity and this injection vanish. Near zero attenuates them; it does not guarantee exact reset. A nonzero new write can still give the updated activation a nonzero value.</p>
      <div class="equation-panel mt-3" role="region" aria-label="Journal shared-gate weight update" tabindex="0"><div set:html={update} /></div>
      <p>S j is the number of cells in block j; e s is each cell’s internal state error from equation 13. Each cell keeps its own sensitivity. The shared input/forget-gate weight update sums their contributions. Error propagation stops after leaving a memory cell or gate, while incoming weights can still learn.</p>
      <details class="trace-arithmetic"><summary>Audit initialization and computational scope</summary><p class="mt-3">Positive forget-gate bias initially puts retention near standard LSTM. The source retains O(1) update time and storage per weight per step, independent of sequence length, and adds gate weights. This is an asymptotic per-weight claim, not constant total model cost or an empirical speedup.</p></details>
      <EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("forget-gate-learning-and-complexity")} />
    </section>}
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="forget-cerg">
      <p class="kicker">Static evidence · continual embedded Reber grammar</p><h2 id="forget-cerg">A learned reset, under a stated criterion.</h2>
      <p>The main CERG task concatenates generated grammar strings without externally imposed segmentation or intermediate state resets. Its grammar still contains B/E symbols. The separately described variant removing those cues is a qualitative extension, not the condition behind Table 2’s percentages.</p>
      {expert ? <>
        <p class="mt-3">The journal uses seven input and seven output units, four two-cell blocks, 424 adjustable weights, and 100 independently initialized networks. Training stops after at most 30,000 streams. Perfect requires correct prediction of ten test streams of 100,000 symbols each.</p>
        <table class="trace-table retention-results mt-4"><caption>Journal Table 2 · perfect solutions over 100 runs · internal p. 12</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Condition</th><th scope="col">Perfect</th><th scope="col">Mean training streams to perfect</th></tr></thead><tbody>{cerg.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.label}</th><td>{row.perfect}</td><td>{row.streams}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
        <table class="trace-table retention-secondary mt-4"><caption>Journal Table 2 · good solutions · separate from perfect</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Condition</th><th scope="col">Good</th></tr></thead><tbody>{cerg.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.label}</th><td>{row.good}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
        <p class="provenance-note mt-3">Good means average error-free test-stream length &gt; 1,000 symbols without meeting perfect. A dash means no perfect solution and no corresponding mean; it is not zero training. The fixed-decay row is the reported selected 0.9 setting, not a full matched sweep. Sequential decay changes the learning-rate schedule as well as using the gate.</p>
        <EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("cerg-protocol")} />
      </> : <p class="mt-3">Across 100 runs, the original cell with teacher-supplied resets reaches 74% perfect solutions. Without those resets, the original cell and the reported fixed-decay setting reach 0%. Learned forget gates reach 18%; with a separate learning-rate decay schedule, 62%. “Perfect” means all ten 100,000-symbol test streams were correct. These are generated symbol streams, not word prediction.</p>}
      <EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("cerg-results")} />
      <p class="mt-3">In an inspected successful network, one block retains the grammar’s P/T distinction across a string and nearly resets at its learned boundary; other blocks reset more often. This is a selected-network analysis, not a behavior measured across all 100 runs.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("cerg-self-reset-analysis")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="forget-cnto">
      <p class="kicker">Static evidence · continual noisy temporal order</p><h2 id="forget-cnto">A second synthetic family, a different unit of success.</h2>
      <p>CNTO concatenates generated temporal-order sequences. The model must classify each from the order of earlier designated symbols. Perfect requires 1,000 successive correct sequences in each of ten test streams; it is not the CERG symbol-level criterion.</p>
      {expert ? <>
        <p class="mt-3">The experiment again uses 100 independently trained networks, with a cap of 100,000 training streams. The journal’s Table 3 uses the following means; they must not be replaced by the technical report’s larger training counts.</p>
        <table class="trace-table retention-results mt-4"><caption>Journal Table 3 · perfect CNTO solutions over 100 runs · internal p. 16</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Condition</th><th scope="col">Perfect</th><th scope="col">Mean training streams to perfect</th></tr></thead><tbody>{cnto.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.label}</th><td>{row.perfect}</td><td>{row.streams}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
        <table class="trace-table retention-secondary mt-4"><caption>Journal Table 3 · mean error-free run for partial solutions</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Condition</th><th scope="col">Sequences</th></tr></thead><tbody>{cnto.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.label}</th><td>{row.partial}</td></tr>)}</tbody></table>
        <p class="provenance-note mt-3">The partial-solution mean describes the non-perfect runs, not all trials or the mean training count. Percentages have no reported confidence intervals or formal significance tests.</p>
      </> : <p class="mt-3">Standard LSTM reaches 0% perfect; learned forget gates reach 24%; adding learning-rate decay reaches 37%. These rates do not establish how the model would learn boundaries in natural language. The three authored retention presets above are separate from these trained experiments.</p>}
      <EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("cnto-results")} />
      <p class="mt-3">On the separate noncontinual control, forget bias 5 gives learning speed similar to standard LSTM; bias 1 takes about three times longer but still meets the task criterion. The tested compatibility result is not a guarantee that forgetting is harmless on every task.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("noncontinual-memory-control")} />
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="forget-versions">
      <p class="kicker">Version audit · one work, three artifacts</p><h2 id="forget-versions">Keep equations and results with their version.</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3"><li><strong>IDSIA-01-99:</strong> institutional issue date 26 January 1999; title-page month January; author bibliography says February. The surviving author file is a 24 February build. That build’s forget-weight derivative differs from the later institutional build and journal.</li><li><strong>ICANN’99:</strong> condensed CERG-only account, printed pp. 850–855. It omits the journal’s fixed-decay row, good/rest columns, and CNTO experiment. Earlier accounts report 375 weights; the journal reports 424.</li><li><strong>Neural Computation 2000:</strong> controls the equations and experiment values on this page. CNTO success rates match the report, but mean training counts differ materially.</li></ul>
      <p class="provenance-note mt-3">All “internal” locators refer to the author reading copy’s pp. 1–19 after its acceptance cover. They do not map to published pp. 2451–2471. Finer ICANN presentation/publication dates remain unresolved.</p>
      <a class="claim-link" href={`/reference/works/${view.work.id}/`}>Inspect the complete nine-claim work record</a>
    </section>
    <section class="reading-block" aria-labelledby="forget-limits">
      <p class="kicker">Boundary · activation reset</p><h2 id="forget-limits">A retention mechanism, within synthetic evidence.</h2>
      <p>The reported evidence concerns two generated continual sequence families. Natural-language modeling and broader real-world boundary discovery are not evaluated here. The topology is not optimized; no released code, learned parameters, or modern replication package makes these tables a reproducible benchmark suite.</p>
      <p class="mt-3">The gate can change carried activation while learned weights persist. Direct cell-state inspection is a separate later extension. Do not treat a forget gate as a peephole, a complete backward gradient, or evidence about catastrophic parameter forgetting.</p><EvidenceDetails evidence={claim("evidence-boundary")} />
    </section>
    <nav class="reading-block" aria-label="Recurrent journey progression"><p class="kicker">Next distinction · state visibility</p><h2>Let a gate inspect state · 2002</h2><p>The next chapter adds direct cell-state-to-gate peepholes. Retaining an activation and letting a gate inspect it are different operations.</p><p class="provenance-note mt-3">Two later chapters and both bridge panels remain queued.</p><p class="mt-4"><a class="button" href={view.next!.href}>Continue to state inspection · 2002 <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></p><p class="mt-4"><a class="claim-link" href={view.previous!.href}>Revisit the original LSTM cell</a></p><p class="mt-3"><a class="claim-link" href={`/journeys/recurrent-memory/${mode}/`}>Return to the {mode} route map</a></p><div class="mt-4"><JourneyMap journey={journey} currentSlug={view.stage.slug} mode={mode} compact /></div></nav>
  </div>
</article>
